#!/usr/bin/env bash
# session — the session CLI: this Claude Code session's identity and its usage
# of the plan's rate limits, with a guard mode for pacing multi-agent jobs.
#
# Shows the 5-hour and weekly (7-day) rate-limit %s + reset countdowns (the caps
# that actually gate the session), plus context-window fill. Source: the cache the
# statusline writes (<data root>/last-status.<login>.json) from its
# stdin `rate_limits`. Reset countdowns and pace caps are computed live.
#
# Multi-account (`session account`): everything limit-related is keyed by the
# LOGIN — the email in $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json — because rate-limit
# windows are per-account. The cache is per-login, and per-session attribution
# only counts sample rows tagged with the invoking login (column 21 of
# session-log.tsv), so accounts never mix. Time tracking (`session time`) stays
# account-agnostic: a session's wall time is real whichever login ran it.
#
# No dollar cost display: subscription plan, so the API-equivalent $ would be
# misleading as money; it is used internally as the attribution weight.
#
# Modes:
#   session           overview (default): invoking session's id · name, the 5h +
#                     weekly limits, context fill, and this session's share
#   session whoami [--id|--name|--json]  identity only: id + auto-title
#   session name <id|prefix> · session id <title-substring>  cross-session
#                     lookup, both directions (snapshots first, then the
#                     transcript store: by file name for an id, through the
#                     title index for a title)
#   session peers [--json]  live sessions on this box: cross-session address
#                     (ListAgents name / SendMessage `to:`) ↔ session id +
#                     title, with messaging-socket reachability
#   session account [list|use <name>|save|rm <name>]  several subscription
#                     logins out of one config dir: list them with each one's
#                     rate-limit headroom (the question you switch on), or swap
#                     the live login in place — effective on the next request,
#                     running sessions included. Add a login by running /login;
#                     the statusline vaults it, and the one it replaces, so
#                     switching back never re-authenticates.
#   session usage [ID] [--json]  one session's tracked in-window burn ($, counted
#                     from the statusline's per-session cost samples) and its
#                     estimated share of the 5h/weekly limits. ID defaults to
#                     the invoking session.
#   session usage --all [--json]  per-session breakdown of all tracked burn in
#                     the current windows, plus totals
#   session time [--all]  per-turn time tracking for today: turns, active time
#                     (sum of start→end wall spans, so idle gaps between turns
#                     are excluded), open/unclosed turns. Data source: the turn
#                     log below.
#   session doctor    whether this install is wired: data root, the CLI on
#                     PATH, the statusline's cache, the turn log, the effective
#                     statusLine for this directory, the session id, the
#                     auto-resume entries, and what this host can do. Each check
#                     is ok, pending (nothing has produced it yet) or FAIL, and
#                     only a FAIL exits 1.
#   session --compact one line: date/time + 5h & weekly %s (token-frugal, always
#                     exits 0 so it can never block a prompt)
#   session --hook    UserPromptSubmit plumbing, warn-gated: silent (no output at
#                     all) while both windows are below USAGE_WARN_PCT. At/above
#                     it, emits that line + a ⚠ advisory wrapped as
#                     hook JSON (hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext +
#                     suppressOutput:true — injected into the model's context,
#                     kept out of the user's transcript). ONE advisory whatever
#                     the window or reset distance — only the lead sentence
#                     names which window(s) crossed: keep working, fan-out
#                     included; the auto-resume waiter (--rewake-waiter below)
#                     is armed by the harness, so no action is asked of the
#                     model (fallback text still instructs a manual `--wait`
#                     when settings.json lacks the asyncRewake entry). Once a
#                     warned window's resets_at passes — or the login switches
#                     to an account that isn't warning — the next turn gets a
#                     one-line refresh notice (no breakdown) telling the model
#                     the cap is fresh and work can resume; per-session warn
#                     state (reset ts + login) lives in sessions/<sid>.warn.
#                     Independently of the gate, the same envelope carries
#                     hookSpecificOutput.sessionTitle ONCE per session, to make
#                     the session addressable by its title: it promotes the
#                     auto-title to the presence registry's `name` — what
#                     ListAgents prints and SendMessage addresses — while that
#                     name is still the app's cwd slug. `claude --name` and
#                     `/rename` set the same field themselves and are never
#                     overwritten; rename is how you change it afterwards.
#   session --rewake-waiter  asyncRewake hook plumbing (second UserPromptSubmit
#                     entry AND second StopFailure entry): the harness
#                     backgrounds it and treats exit 2 as "wake the model with
#                     stderr as a system reminder" (task-notification channel —
#                     wakes even an idle session; verified on 2.1.233 for
#                     UserPromptSubmit; StopFailure rides the same
#                     schema-generic asyncRewake field). From UserPromptSubmit:
#                     below the warn threshold every spawn exits 0 immediately;
#                     above it the first spawn per session becomes the waiter
#                     (pidfile-deduped). From StopFailure: arms only when the
#                     turn died on a usage cap (error ~ rate_limit), and then
#                     regardless of the warn threshold — the one path that
#                     covers a cap arriving mid-turn with no prompt ever
#                     submitted in the warned band. The waiter sleeps to the
#                     earliest warned reset and exits 2 = auto-resume — also on
#                     a mid-wait login switch, detected within ~1s via an
#                     inotify watch on the config dir (15s polling without
#                     inotifywait). Exits 0 without waiting under a `claude -p`
#                     parent: the harness only backgrounds asyncRewake for
#                     interactive/streaming sessions, so there the hook would
#                     run synchronously and block the print run.
#                     Side effect every turn, gated or not: appends this turn's
#                     START event to the turn log.
#   session --turn-end  Stop-hook plumbing: appends the turn's END event to the
#                     turn log; always exits 0 silently (a Stop hook's exit 2
#                     would force the agent to keep going).
#
# Turn log (<data root>/turn-log.tsv, "ts sid ev prompt_id detail
# agent_id"; events s/e/f/x/a/z/c/p — see the hook-plumbing dispatcher below):
# turn wall time = e minus s, which includes tool execution —
# cost.total_duration_ms can't give this (it's session wall clock, ticking
# through idle gaps; verified empirically), and total_api_duration_ms excludes
# tool time. Hook-fed, so headless `claude -p` runs get turn records too,
# without a statusline. Gaps between an e and the next s are unattended time.
#   session --json    raw fields for scripting
#   session --guard   pacing gate: prints OK/PAUSE, exits 0 (ok) or 3 (pause)
#   session --wait [5h|week|guard]  block, then print one line and exit 0.
#                     5h/week (default 5h): until that window resets. guard:
#                     until the pace guard (`--guard`, same FIVE_GUARD/WEEK_GUARD
#                     config) would pass — sleeps to the computed earliest pass
#                     time rather than polling. Run in the background (e.g. Bash
#                     run_in_background) so the exit notifies you exactly at the
#                     reset/pass — cleaner than polling or ScheduleWakeup hops.
#                     A login switch mid-wait exits at once: the switch is the
#                     wake-up (the old login's countdown no longer applies).
#   session --file PATH  read a specific cache file
#
# Guard config — set EACH window independently (FIVE_GUARD / WEEK_GUARD) to:
#   linear  (default)  pause if used% > 100*x              (x = elapsed fraction)
#   sqrt               concave/eased: pause if used% > 100*sqrt(x)  — permissive
#                      early (fixes the just-after-reset strictness), tightens late
#   pow:P              general power curve: cap = 100*x^P (0<P<=1 concave; P=1 is
#                      linear; smaller P = more early slack). `sqrt` == pow:0.5
#   <int>              flat threshold: pause if used% > N   (e.g. 80)
#   off                disabled: never pause on this window
# e.g.  WEEK_GUARD=sqrt FIVE_GUARD=linear  |  FIVE_GUARD=off WEEK_GUARD=90
# Window sizes for the curves: FIVE_WINDOW (s, 18000), WEEK_WINDOW (s, 604800).
set -uo pipefail

# Every path, every host facility and the platform layer come from the lib
# beside this script. The walk is POSIX on purpose: `readlink -f` is GNU-only,
# and this file is reached through a symlink on PATH on every machine.
# CDPATH first: `cd` consults it for any relative path, and BASH_SOURCE is
# relative whenever this file is invoked as one (`bash <dir>/session`). A
# CDPATH in the environment would then resolve the walk somewhere else entirely
# — and print the directory it chose, which lands inside SESSION_HOME.
CDPATH=
_src="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
_n=0
# Bounded like realpath_of: a symlink loop is a configuration mistake, not a
# reason to spin forever inside a hook.
while [ -L "$_src" ] && [ "$_n" -lt 40 ]; do
  _dir=$(cd -P "$(dirname "$_src")" && pwd)
  _src=$(ls -ld "$_src" | sed 's/.*-> //')
  case "$_src" in /*) ;; *) _src="$_dir/$_src";; esac
  _n=$(( _n + 1 ))
done
SESSION_HOME=$(cd -P "$(dirname "$_src")" && pwd)
. "$SESSION_HOME/lib/common.sh" || { echo "session: cannot read $SESSION_HOME/lib/common.sh" >&2; exit 1; }
. "$SESSION_HOME/lib/account.sh" || { echo "session: cannot read $SESSION_HOME/lib/account.sh" >&2; exit 1; }

# cache resolution is deferred until after arg parsing: it depends on the login
# (a jq read of .claude.json) which the hot plumbing paths (--focus-mark,
# --turn-end, …) never need — they exit before it.
cache=""
tlog="$SESSION_TLOG"
flog="$SESSION_FLOG"
panedir="$SESSION_PANEDIR"
slog="$SESSION_SLOG"
snapdir="$SESSION_SNAPDIR"
uarchive="$SESSION_ARCHIVE"
# The pruner moves older rows out to <data root>/archive/<same basename>, which
# used to put anything past the horizon out of reach — `session time --date` on
# such a day reported "nothing logged" for a day that was in fact worked. Every
# reader sorts by timestamp anyway, so covering all of history is just a matter
# of handing it both halves. `log_srcs <live> <since>` echoes the files a read
# back to <since> needs, oldest first.
#
# The archive is skipped only where the live file PROVABLY covers <since>: the
# request is inside the retention floor, AND the live file's first row predates
# <since> by a day (a file whose first row is newer cannot hold that day's
# earlier rows — a prune, a fresh install or a moved data root all produce
# exactly that shape, and reading the live file alone would report the day as
# empty). The decision is per FILE, never per row: the reader's unclosed-turn
# recovery tracks each session's cumulative prefix (prev[] in the "g" pass), so
# a half-read file would change the answers rather than just shorten them.
log_srcs() {
  local live=$1 since="${2:-0}" arch="$uarchive/${1##*/}" floor first
  floor=$(( $(now_epoch) - SESSION_LIVE_FLOOR_DAYS * 86400 ))
  if [ -s "$live" ] && [ "$since" -ge "$floor" ]; then
    # printf %d, not print $1+0: the focus log's stamps carry milliseconds, and
    # awk renders a non-integral one through OFMT as 1.78779e+09.
    first=$(awk -F'\t' 'NR==1{printf "%d\n", $1; exit}' "$live")
    # > 0, not just non-empty: printf %d of a blank or non-numeric first field
    # is 0, and 0 predates every midnight — one degenerate row at the head of
    # the file would otherwise "prove" that the live log covers all of history
    # and hide the archive completely.
    if [ -n "$first" ] && [ "$first" -gt 0 ] && [ "$first" -le $(( since - 86400 )) ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$live"
      return 0
    fi
  fi
  [ -s "$arch" ] && printf '%s\n' "$arch"
  [ -s "$live" ] && printf '%s\n' "$live"
  return 0
}
# Attention accrues in two distinct steps, which used to share one number.
# BRIDGE: how long a silence between two interactions still counts as one
# continuous stretch — you paused to read, you did not leave. TAIL: how much is
# credited after the LAST interaction of a stretch, where nothing says you were
# still there. Sharing one value means every stretch you walk away from books a
# full bridge's worth, which on a fragmented day is most of the total: 13
# stretches x 10 min is over two hours of pure grace. TAIL defaults to BRIDGE,
# so the figure is unchanged until someone asks for a stricter one.
ATTEND_GRACE="$SESSION_ATTEND_GRACE"   # bridge: silence still counted as one stretch
ATTEND_TAIL="$SESSION_ATTEND_TAIL"     # tail: credit past the last interaction
FIVE_GUARD="${FIVE_GUARD:-linear}"
WEEK_GUARD="${WEEK_GUARD:-linear}"
FIVE_WINDOW="${FIVE_WINDOW:-18000}"
WEEK_WINDOW="${WEEK_WINDOW:-604800}"
# The used-% at or above which --hook injects its line and advisory and
# --rewake-waiter arms. One default, here, rather than a copy at each use site:
# the copies drift, and the consumer left holding the stale one gates at a
# threshold nobody chose. The environment-wins form is the lib's, and is what
# lets a session.conf line set this with no further plumbing — the conf being
# the only channel that reaches a hook, which inherits no shell environment.
# Above 100 disables the hook's output entirely.
USAGE_WARN_PCT="${USAGE_WARN_PCT:-90}"

# Duration formatters, shared by the overview and `session account`.
hm()  { local s=$(( $1<0 ? 0 : $1 )); printf '%dh%02dm' $(( s/3600 ))  $(( (s%3600)/60 )); }
# Days+hours, but ROUNDED, and h+m below a day. Flooring the hour drops up to 59
# minutes, which reads as a whole hour short: 58m of weekly headroom printed
# "0d00h" — indistinguishable from "already reset" — while the built-in /usage
# correctly showed the hour, and 6d08h50m prints "6d08h", implying a reset an hour
# early. Below a day it falls through to hm() so a sub-hour remainder can never
# render as a zero.
dh()  { local s=$(( $1<0 ? 0 : $1 )) h
        (( s < 86400 )) && { hm "$s"; return; }
        h=$(( (s + 1800) / 3600 ))
        printf '%dd%02dh' $(( h/24 )) $(( h%24 )); }

# ── Session identity (`session whoami`) ──────────────────────────
# Pane-safe by construction: the id comes from $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, which
# Claude Code exports into each session's own process tree; every concurrent
# tmux pane runs a distinct claude process with its own value. Fallback: walk
# up to the nearest ancestor that has it (env var not inherited is unusual).
resolve_sid() {
  local sid pid ppid v
  sid="${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}"
  if [ -z "$sid" ]; then
    pid=$$
    while [ "${pid:-0}" -gt 1 ]; do
      ppid=$(proc_ppid "$pid") || break
      [ -z "$ppid" ] && break
      v=$(proc_env "$ppid" CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)
      [ -n "$v" ] && { sid="$v"; break; }
      pid="$ppid"
    done
  fi
  [ -n "$sid" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$sid"
}
transcript_of() {  # $1=sid -> its transcript path (empty if none readable)
  local cfg jsonl
  cfg="$SESSION_CFG"
  [ -d "$cfg/projects" ] || return 0
  jsonl=$(find "$cfg/projects" -name "$1.jsonl" -print -quit)
  { [ -n "$jsonl" ] && [ -r "$jsonl" ]; } && printf '%s' "$jsonl"
}
# A session's title, from its transcript under
# $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<sid>.jsonl. Two kinds of entry
# carry one and the app prefers the custom over the auto-generated one, so this
# does too: {"type":"custom-title","customTitle":".."} is what `/rename` (and a
# hook-set sessionTitle) writes, {"type":"ai-title","aiTitle":".."} the app's
# own summary, re-appended as the conversation moves. Precedence, not recency —
# the auto-titler keeps writing after a rename, and the rename is what the user
# sees in the prompt box and what peers address the session by.
session_title() {  # $1=sid ; empty output if no title yet
  local jsonl line
  jsonl=$(transcript_of "$1")
  [ -n "$jsonl" ] || return 0
  line=$(grep -E '"type":"(custom-title|ai-title)"' "$jsonl" \
    | awk '/"type":"custom-title"/ {c=$0} /"type":"ai-title"/ {a=$0}
           END {print (c != "" ? c : a)}')
  [ -n "$line" ] || return 0
  printf '%s' "$line" | jq -r '.customTitle // .aiTitle // empty'
}
# The FIRST auto-title, for the one-shot peer-name promotion in the hook below.
# Read from a bounded prefix: the app writes the title right after the opening
# exchange (over the 1,492 titled session transcripts of Aug 2026 the entry sat
# p50 32 KB / p99 386 KB / max 1.4 MB in, so the cap has never truncated a real
# one), and the bound exists for the other shape — the ~4% of sessions that
# never get a title at all, where an unbounded scan would re-read a transcript
# that can reach tens of MB on every single prompt.
TITLE_SCAN_BYTES=${TITLE_SCAN_BYTES:-2000000}
first_title() {  # $1=sid -> its first ai-title (empty if none in the prefix)
  local jsonl line
  jsonl=$(transcript_of "$1")
  [ -n "$jsonl" ] || return 0
  # grep reads a process substitution rather than a pipe: `head | grep -m1`
  # SIGPIPEs head on a match, and under pipefail that turns a HIT into a
  # non-zero pipeline that reads as "no title".
  line=$(grep -m1 '"type":"ai-title"' < <(head -c "$TITLE_SCAN_BYTES" "$jsonl")) || return 0
  printf '%s' "$line" | jq -r '.aiTitle // empty'
}
whoami_usage() {
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Usage: session whoami [--id | --name | --json | -h]
  (no args)  two lines: "id:" and "name:"
  --id       print only the session UUID
  --name     print only the auto-generated title
  --json     print {"id": "...", "name": "..."}

Run from inside a Claude Code session (any tmux pane / any subshell of it).
EOF
}
whoami_main() {
  local sid name
  case "${1:-}" in
    -h|--help) whoami_usage; exit 0 ;;
    ""|--id|--name|--json) ;;
    *) whoami_usage; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  if ! sid=$(resolve_sid); then
    echo "session whoami: not inside a Claude Code session (CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID unset)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  name=$(session_title "$sid")
  case "${1:-}" in
    --id)   printf '%s\n' "$sid" ;;
    --name) printf '%s\n' "$name" ;;
    --json) jq -cn --arg id "$sid" --arg name "$name" '{id: $id, name: $name}' ;;
    "")     printf 'id:   %s\nname: %s\n' "$sid" "${name:-<untitled>}" ;;
  esac
  exit 0
}
[ "${1:-}" = whoami ] && { shift; whoami_main "$@"; }

# ── Cross-session lookup (`session name <id>` ↔ `session id <query>`) ────────
# whoami only reports the INVOKING session; these resolve any other one, both
# directions. Fast path: the usage snapshots — every session that rendered a
# statusline in the last 8 days, i.e. exactly the ids the usage/time tables and
# the daily brief show. Fallback when nothing matches there: the transcript
# store under $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects — an id finds its file by NAME
# (cheap); a title query reads the title index below.
snap_pairs() {  # "sid<TAB>name" for every usage snapshot
  set -- "$snapdir"/*.json
  [ -e "$1" ] || return 0
  jq -r '[input_filename, (.session_name // "")] | @tsv' "$@" 2>/dev/null \
    | awk -F'\t' '{ sub(/.*\//,"",$1); sub(/\.json$/,"",$1); print $1 "\t" $2 }'
}
title_of_file() {  # $1=transcript path -> its title (empty if none)
  local line                                   # same custom-over-auto precedence as session_title
  line=$(grep -E '"type":"(custom-title|ai-title)"' "$1" 2>/dev/null \
    | awk '/"type":"custom-title"/ {c=$0} /"type":"ai-title"/ {a=$0}
           END {print (c != "" ? c : a)}')
  [ -n "$line" ] && printf '%s' "$line" | jq -r '.customTitle // .aiTitle // empty'
}
# The title index: <data root>/title-index.tsv, "sid<TAB>title" for every
# titled transcript in the store, with session_title's custom-over-auto
# precedence. A title query used to grep the whole store on every snapshot
# miss and then run one jq per titled transcript — measured 2026-09 on an
# 8 GB store of 15k transcripts: 45 s of grep plus 36 s for 4,300 jq spawns,
# so one lookup cost over a minute and four in a row outlived a tool's
# timeout. The index is built once (the same scan, through a single jq; on
# that store 47 s) and after that a
# query re-reads only the transcripts modified since the index was written: a
# `find -newer` over the store and a grep of a handful of files. The index's
# mtime is the moment its scan STARTED, so a transcript written while the scan
# ran is re-read next time rather than missed until its next edit; a row whose
# transcript is gone is dropped on the next refresh. The temp dir sits under
# the data root so the final mv is a same-filesystem rename and a concurrent
# reader never sees a half-written index. A refresh that cannot write (data
# root read-only, disk full) says so on stderr and the last index is served
# as it stands; only a store with no index at all fails the lookup.
title_index() {  # refreshes the index and prints its path; nothing without one
  local cfgp="$SESSION_CFG/projects" idx="$SESSION_DATA/title-index.tsv" work
  [ -d "$cfgp" ] || return 1
  if ! mkdir -p "$SESSION_DATA" || ! work=$(mktemp -d "$SESSION_DATA/.tidx.XXXXXX"); then
    [ -f "$idx" ] && printf '%s\n' "$idx"
    return
  fi
  : > "$work/stamp"
  find "$cfgp" -name '*.jsonl' > "$work/all"
  if [ -f "$idx" ]; then
    find "$cfgp" -name '*.jsonl' -newer "$idx" > "$work/changed"
  else
    echo "session id: building the title index over the transcript store ($(du -sh "$cfgp" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)) — once; later queries read it" >&2
    cp "$work/all" "$work/changed"
  fi
  # A grep pass over the changed transcripts (xargs batches a full-store build
  # into a few invocations), then one jq over all of its output, each title
  # entry becoming "sid<TAB>kind<TAB>title". The entry's fields are taken by
  # regex rather than by parsing the line as JSON: an entry can share its line
  # with the record before it (seen once in a real store), and one unparseable
  # line must not abort the rest — hence the `try` on the unescape too. The
  # title is stored as `session name` shows it, except that a tab or newline
  # inside it becomes a space so the file stays one row per transcript; an
  # escaping encoder here would make a backslash in a title unmatchable.
  if [ -s "$work/changed" ]; then
    tr '\n' '\0' < "$work/changed" \
      | xargs -0 grep -asH -E '"type":"(custom-title|ai-title)"' \
      | jq -rR '
          capture("^(?<f>.*\\.jsonl):(?<l>.*)$")
          | (.f | sub(".*/"; "") | sub("\\.jsonl$"; "")) as $sid
          | (.l | capture("\"type\":\"(?<k>custom-title|ai-title)\",\"(customTitle|aiTitle)\":\"(?<t>(\\\\.|[^\"\\\\])*)\""))
          | [$sid, .k, ("\"" + .t + "\"" | try fromjson | gsub("[\\t\\n\\r]"; " "))] | join("\t")'
  fi > "$work/titles"
  # Merge: live transcripts (A), the ones re-read (C), their fresh entries (N),
  # the previous index (O). An old row survives only for a live transcript that
  # was not re-read; a re-read transcript's row is whatever its entries say now.
  {
    awk '{ sub(/.*\//, ""); sub(/\.jsonl$/, ""); print "A\t" $0 }' "$work/all"
    awk '{ sub(/.*\//, ""); sub(/\.jsonl$/, ""); print "C\t" $0 }' "$work/changed"
    awk '{ print "N\t" $0 }' "$work/titles"
    [ -f "$idx" ] && awk '{ print "O\t" $0 }' "$idx"
  } | awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' '
      $1 == "A" { live[$2] = 1; next }
      $1 == "C" { changed[$2] = 1; next }
      $1 == "N" { if ($3 == "custom-title") c[$2] = $4; else a[$2] = $4; seen[$2] = 1; next }
      $1 == "O" { if (!($2 in changed) && ($2 in live)) print $2, $3; next }
      END { for (s in seen) { t = (s in c) ? c[s] : a[s]; if (t != "") print s, t } }
    ' | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$work/new"
  mv -f "$work/new" "$idx" && touch -r "$work/stamp" "$idx"
  rm -rf "$work"
  [ -f "$idx" ] && printf '%s\n' "$idx"
}
# The query reaches awk through ENVIRON, not -v: a -v value has its backslash
# escapes interpreted, so a title holding a path like C:\Users could never be
# matched by typing it.
lk_filter() {  # $1=name|id  $2=query : keep matching "sid<TAB>name" rows
  if [ "$1" = name ]; then LK_Q="$2" awk -F'\t' 'index($1, ENVIRON["LK_Q"])==1'
  else LK_Q="$2" awk -F'\t' 'index(tolower($2), tolower(ENVIRON["LK_Q"]))>0'; fi
}
lookup_main() {  # $1=name|id  $2=query
  local lmode=$1 q=${2:-} m n cfgp f s
  if [ -z "$q" ] || [ "$q" = -h ] || [ "$q" = --help ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Usage: session name <session-id | id-prefix>   -> that session's title
       session id   <title-substring>          -> that session's id (case-insensitive)
One match prints the bare value, so both are scriptable, e.g.
  agent <dir> -r "$(session id 'dead-link')"
Several matches print "id<TAB>name" lines instead. Sessions active in the last
8 days resolve off the usage snapshots; older ones off the transcript store —
an id by file name, a title through an index built on first use (one scan of
the store) and refreshed per query for the transcripts modified since.
EOF
    [ -z "$q" ] && exit 2; exit 0
  fi
  m=$(snap_pairs | lk_filter "$lmode" "$q")
  if [ -z "$m" ]; then
    cfgp="$SESSION_CFG/projects"
    if [ -d "$cfgp" ] && [ "$lmode" = name ]; then
      m=$(find "$cfgp" -name "$q*.jsonl" | while IFS= read -r f; do
            s=$(basename "$f" .jsonl)
            printf '%s\t%s\n' "$s" "$(title_of_file "$f")"
          done | LC_ALL=C sort -u)
    elif [ -d "$cfgp" ]; then
      f=$(title_index) && m=$(lk_filter id "$q" < "$f")
    fi
  fi
  [ -n "$m" ] || { echo "session $lmode: no session matching '$q'" >&2; exit 1; }
  if [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$m" | wc -l)" -eq 1 ]; then
    if [ "$lmode" = name ]; then
      s=${m%%$'\t'*}; n=${m#*$'\t'}
      [ -n "$n" ] || n=$(session_title "$s")   # snapshot exists but untitled yet
      printf '%s\n' "${n:-<untitled>}"
    else
      printf '%s\n' "${m%%$'\t'*}"
    fi
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$m"
  fi
  exit 0
}
case "${1:-}" in name|id) lookup_main "$@" ;; esac

# ── Peer sessions (`session peers`) ──────────────────────────────
# The bridge between ListAgents' cross-session addresses and session ids.
# Claude Code (2.1.226+) registers every live process at
# $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions/<pid>.json — peer name (the SendMessage `to:`),
# sessionId, tmux pane, messaging socket path. ListAgents hides a session whose
# socket is missing, so the REACH column makes that state visible instead of the
# session just being absent. Dead pids' leftover records are skipped, never
# deleted (the registry is Claude Code's, not ours).
peers_main() {
  local arg=${1:-} regdir mysid f rec pid sock reach sid pname pstatus ptmux title this rows=""
  case "$arg" in
    ""|--json) ;;
    -h|--help)
      cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Usage: session peers [--json]
Live Claude Code sessions on this box, one per row:
  NAME     cross-session address (the row name ListAgents shows = SendMessage `to:`).
           Normally the session's title — `session --hook` promotes it there once
           the app has generated one; `/rename <name>` sets it yourself, any time
  SESSION  8-char session-id prefix (feeds `session name`, `agent <dir> -r`, …)
  REACH    yes = messaging socket live, ListAgents shows it; no = registered
           without a socket — invisible to ListAgents until that session restarts
--json prints the same rows with full session ids.
EOF
      exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "session peers: unknown arg '$arg' (--json or -h)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  regdir="$SESSION_CFG/sessions"
  mysid=$(resolve_sid || true)
  for f in "$regdir"/*.json; do
    [ -e "$f" ] || break
    rec=$(jq -c '{pid,sessionId,name,status,tmux,sock:(.messagingSocketPath//"")}' "$f" 2>/dev/null) || continue
    pid=$(jq -r '.pid' <<<"$rec")
    [ "$pid" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || continue
    kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || continue
    IFS=$'\t' read -r sid pname pstatus ptmux sock < <(jq -r '[.sessionId,.name,.status,.tmux,.sock]|@tsv' <<<"$rec")
    reach=no; [ -n "$sock" ] && [ -S "$sock" ] && reach=yes
    title=$(session_title "$sid")
    this=""; [ -n "$mysid" ] && [ "$sid" = "$mysid" ] && this=" ←this"
    rows+="$pname	$sid	$pstatus	$reach	$ptmux	${title:-<untitled>}$this"$'\n'
  done
  [ -n "$rows" ] || { echo "session peers: no live sessions registered under $regdir" >&2; exit 1; }
  if [ "$arg" = --json ]; then
    printf '%s' "$rows" | jq -Rn \
      '[inputs | split("\t") | {name: .[0], session_id: .[1], status: .[2],
        reachable: (.[3]=="yes"), tmux: .[4], title: (.[5]|sub(" ←this$";""))}]'
  else
    # `column -t` squeezes repeated delimiters, so an empty field (a session
    # that has not written `status` yet) would shift every later column left;
    # fill the blanks first.
    { printf 'NAME\tSESSION\tSTATUS\tREACH\tTMUX\tTITLE\n'
      printf '%s' "$rows" | awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' \
        '{ $2=substr($2,1,8); for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "") $i="-"; print }'
    } | column -t -s'	'
  fi
  exit 0
}
[ "${1:-}" = peers ] && { shift; peers_main "$@"; }

# ── Reboot / resume (`session reboot`, `session resume`) ─────────
# A reboot takes the tmux server and every Claude session in it, and the
# presence registry ($CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions/*.json) is pruned at startup:
# afterwards only the new session's record is left, so the set to restore has to
# be written down BEFORE going down. `session reboot` snapshots it to the queue
# below; a boot unit replays it with `session resume` (see README.md).
resumeq="$SESSION_RESUME_QUEUE"

# Live interactive sessions -> "sid<TAB>cwd". One session id can hold several
# registry records (one per process stream), so collapse to the newest per id
# before testing the pid.
live_sessions() {
  local regdir f found=0
  regdir="$SESSION_CFG/sessions"
  for f in "$regdir"/*.json; do [ -e "$f" ] && { found=1; break; }; done
  [ "$found" = 1 ] || return 0
  jq -rs '[.[] | select(.kind == "interactive")]
          | group_by(.sessionId) | map(max_by(.updatedAt))
          | .[] | [.pid, .sessionId, (.cwd // "")] | @tsv' "$regdir"/*.json 2>/dev/null \
    | while IFS=$'\t' read -r pid sid cwd; do
        kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && printf '%s\t%s\n' "$sid" "$cwd"
      done
}

# The directory a session was launched in — what restoring it has to reproduce.
# Transcripts live under projects/<slug>/, the slug being that directory with
# every `/` and `.` turned into `-`. A transcript entry's own "cwd" is only the
# shell's cwd at that moment and wanders into subdirs and worktrees (the
# registry's does too), so the launch dir is the recorded cwd that slugs back to
# the transcript's own directory. $2, the registry cwd, covers a session that
# recorded none; landing in the wrong directory only misplaces the session,
# since `claude --resume <id>` resolves the id from anywhere.
resume_dir() {  # $1=sid [$2=fallback] -> launch dir (empty if undecidable)
  local jsonl slug c
  jsonl=$(transcript_of "$1")
  if [ -n "$jsonl" ]; then
    slug=$(basename "$(dirname "$jsonl")")
    while read -r c; do
      [ "${c//[\/.]/-}" = "$slug" ] && { printf '%s' "$c"; return 0; }
    done < <(grep -ao '"cwd":"[^"]*"' "$jsonl" | cut -d'"' -f4 | sort -u)
  fi
  { [ -n "${2:-}" ] && [ -d "$2" ]; } && printf '%s' "$2"
  return 0
}

# Sessions with transcript activity in the last $1 hours, as queue rows. Only
# top-level ones: subagents get "agent-*" ids rather than UUIDs.
scan_sessions() {  # $1=hours
  local f sid dir title
  # BSD find has no -printf, so the mtime is read per file. The ordering is
  # load-bearing: the newest session has to be the first row offered.
  find "$SESSION_CFG/projects" -name '*.jsonl' -mmin "-$(( $1 * 60 ))" 2>/dev/null \
    | while IFS= read -r f; do
        # A transcript can vanish between find and stat, and an unreadable one
        # cannot be resumed anyway. Skipping keeps the ordering a real ordering:
        # an empty field sorts as 0 and would offer the newest session last.
        m=$(mtime_of "$f") && [ -n "$m" ] || continue
        printf '%s %s\n' "$m" "$f"
      done \
    | sort -rn | cut -d' ' -f2- \
    | while read -r f; do
        sid=$(basename "$f" .jsonl)
        [[ $sid =~ ^[0-9a-f]{8}(-[0-9a-f]{4}){3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$ ]] || continue
        dir=$(resume_dir "$sid"); [ -n "$dir" ] || continue
        title=$(session_title "$sid")
        printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$sid" "$dir" "${title:-}"
      done
}

ensure_main_session() {
  # Optional, host-configured: a script to run before the main session is
  # (re)created — SESSION_TMUX_MAIN_GUARD, empty everywhere it is not wanted.
  [ -n "$SESSION_TMUX_MAIN_GUARD" ] && [ -x "$SESSION_TMUX_MAIN_GUARD" ] \
    && "$SESSION_TMUX_MAIN_GUARD" >/dev/null 2>&1
  tmux has-session -t '=main' 2>/dev/null && return 0
  tmux new-session -d -s main -n shell
  tmux set-option -w -t '=main:shell' automatic-rename off
  tmux select-pane -t '=main:shell' -T shell
}

# One tmux window per restored session. Deliberately not the `agent` shell
# function: its outside-tmux branch ends in an attach, which neither a batch nor
# a boot unit can do. The window command runs under `bash -lc` so PATH,
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR and the rest come from the login profile however the tmux
# server was started — at boot a unit starts it, with none of a login shell's
# environment.
spawn_resume_window() {  # $1=dir $2=sid -> window name
  local base name existing i=2 inner
  base=$(basename "$1"); name="$base"
  existing=$(tmux list-windows -t '=main' -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null || true)
  while grep -Fqx "$name" <<<"$existing"; do name="$base-$i"; i=$((i + 1)); done
  inner="cd $(printf '%q' "$1") && exec claude --resume $2"
  tmux new-window -d -t '=main' -n "$name" "bash -lc $(printf '%q' "$inner")" || return 1
  printf '%s' "$name"
}

reboot_main() {
  local yes=0 dry=0 sid cwd dir title rows="" n=0
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -y|--yes) yes=1 ;;
      -n|--dry-run) dry=1 ;;
      -h|--help)
        cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Usage: session reboot [-n|--dry-run] [-y|--yes]
Snapshot every live interactive session, then reboot the box. The sessions come
back on their own at boot if a boot unit runs `session resume` (see the README).
  -n  write the snapshot and print it, without rebooting
  -y  skip the confirmation prompt (required when stdin is not a terminal)
EOF
        exit 0 ;;
      *) echo "session reboot: unknown arg '$1' (-n, -y or -h)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  # reboot_main itself invokes no tmux: the sessions it snapshots live in the
  # presence registry. What needs tmux is the REPLAY, so a real reboot on a host
  # without it would write a queue nothing can consume. -h answers above, and -n
  # writes the snapshot without rebooting, so neither is refused.
  if [ "$dry" != 1 ]; then
    command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "session reboot: needs tmux — the snapshot it writes is only replayable by \`session resume\`, which reopens each session in a tmux window" >&2; exit 1; }
  fi
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r sid cwd; do
    dir=$(resume_dir "$sid" "$cwd")
    if [ -z "$dir" ]; then
      echo "session reboot: no launch directory for ${sid:0:8}, skipping" >&2
      continue
    fi
    title=$(session_title "$sid")
    rows+="$sid	$dir	${title:-<untitled>}"$'\n'
    n=$((n + 1))
  done < <(live_sessions)
  if [ "$n" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "session reboot: no live interactive sessions to snapshot" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$resumeq")"
  if ! printf '%s' "$rows" > "$resumeq"; then
    echo "session reboot: could not write $resumeq — not rebooting" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  printf 'Snapshot -> %s\n\n' "$resumeq"
  { printf 'SESSION\tDIRECTORY\tTITLE\n'
    printf '%s' "$rows" | awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' '{ $1=substr($1,1,8); print }'
  } | column -t -s'	'
  printf '\n%s session(s) will be restored after the reboot.\n' "$n"
  [ "$dry" = 1 ] && exit 0
  if [ "$yes" != 1 ]; then
    if [ ! -t 0 ]; then
      echo "session reboot: refusing to reboot without a terminal; pass -y to confirm" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    read -r -p "Reboot now? [y/N] " reply
    case "$reply" in y|Y|yes|YES) ;; *) echo "Aborted (snapshot kept)."; exit 1 ;; esac
  fi
  reboot_cmd
}

resume_main() {
  local dry=0 scan=0 hours=48 sid dir title rows="" live name n=0 skipped=0 kept=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -n|--dry-run) dry=1 ;;
      --scan) scan=1
              if [ $# -gt 1 ] && [ "${2#-}" = "${2:-}" ]; then hours="$2"; shift; fi ;;
      -h|--help)
        cat >&2 <<'EOF'
Usage: session resume [--scan [HOURS]] [-n|--dry-run]
Reopen sessions in tmux windows under `main`, one window each.
  (no args)      replay the snapshot `session reboot` left, then clear it
  --scan [H]     ignore the snapshot; offer every top-level session with
                 transcript activity in the last H hours (default 48)
  -n             print what would be reopened, change nothing
Already-running sessions are skipped, so re-running this is safe.
EOF
        exit 0 ;;
      *) echo "session resume: unknown arg '$1' (--scan, -n or -h)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  # -h and -n answer without tmux; opening the windows is what needs it.
  if [ "$dry" != 1 ]; then
    command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "session resume: needs tmux (it reopens each session in a tmux window)" >&2; exit 1; }
  fi
  if [ "$scan" = 1 ]; then
    rows=$(scan_sessions "$hours")
  elif [ -s "$resumeq" ]; then
    rows=$(cat "$resumeq")
  else
    echo "session resume: nothing queued ($resumeq)." >&2
    echo "Use 'session resume --scan [HOURS]' to reopen recent sessions instead." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  live=$(live_sessions | cut -f1)
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r sid dir title; do
    [ -n "$sid" ] || continue
    if grep -qx "$sid" <<<"$live"; then skipped=$((skipped + 1)); continue; fi
    if [ ! -d "$dir" ]; then
      echo "session resume: $dir is gone, cannot reopen ${sid:0:8} there" >&2
      kept+="$sid	$dir	$title"$'\n'
      continue
    fi
    if [ "$dry" = 1 ]; then
      printf 'would reopen  %s  %s  %s\n' "${sid:0:8}" "$dir" "${title:-<untitled>}"
    else
      ensure_main_session
      if ! name=$(spawn_resume_window "$dir" "$sid"); then
        echo "session resume: tmux would not open a window for ${sid:0:8}" >&2
        kept+="$sid	$dir	$title"$'\n'
        continue
      fi
      printf 'reopened  %-10s  %s  %s\n' "$name" "${sid:0:8}" "${title:-<untitled>}"
    fi
    n=$((n + 1))
  done <<< "$rows"
  [ "$skipped" -gt 0 ] && printf '%s already running, skipped.\n' "$skipped"
  # Consume only what reopened: an entry that failed stays queued, so a deleted
  # worktree cannot quietly take a session's only record with it. Reopen one by
  # hand with `agent <dir> -r <id>` from anywhere — the id resolves globally.
  if [ "$dry" != 1 ] && [ "$scan" != 1 ]; then
    if [ -n "$kept" ]; then
      printf '%s' "$kept" > "$resumeq"
      printf '%s session(s) left queued in %s\n' \
        "$(printf '%s' "$kept" | grep -c '')" "$resumeq" >&2
    else
      rm -f "$resumeq"
    fi
  fi
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] || { echo "session resume: nothing to reopen." >&2; exit 1; }
  exit 0
}
[ "${1:-}" = reboot ] && { shift; reboot_main "$@"; }
[ "${1:-}" = resume ] && { shift; resume_main "$@"; }

# ── Accounts (`session account …`) ───────────────────────────────
# Several subscription logins, ONE config dir. The live login is
# $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.credentials.json's `claudeAiOauth` (auth) plus
# .claude.json's `oauthAccount` (identity); a vault at
# $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/accounts/<email>.json keeps a copy of every login seen.
#
# Switching in place (rather than one config dir per account) keeps everything
# else shared: transcripts, memory, settings, MCP servers and history stay put,
# so `--resume` and every hook keep working across a switch.
#
# A swap reaches RUNNING sessions, on their next request — no restart, no
# re-auth, which is the whole point when a cap dies mid-task. Mechanism, read
# out of the 2.1.220 bundle: the token is memoized (`ms.cache`), but building
# an API client awaits `Dy()` → `HHg()`, which stats .credentials.json and, if
# the mtime moved, calls `EW()` to clear that memo — so the next request reads
# the new file. On Linux the plaintext backend's `read()` is an uncached
# readFileSync (the cache with a TTL belongs to the macOS keychain path).
# Measured end to end 2026-07-31 on a controlled session: pre-swap it reported
# 5h 36%/wk 66%, and the very first request after the swap reported the other
# account's 5h 4%/wk 100%.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing:
#   • the switch is BOX-WIDE, not per session — every `claude` here shares this
#     config dir, so they all move to the new login on their next request;
#   • between requests the statusline just repeats the last API response, so
#     the displayed %s only catch up on the next turn. (Reading one of those
#     stale repeats is what first made this look like it had not switched.)
#
# Data-loss safety, since the whole point is that `/login` used to destroy the
# login it replaced:
#   • the statusline autosaves the live login into the vault every render whose
#     .credentials.json mtime moved, so an accidental /login is captured within
#     ~10s and the REPLACED login is already safe (it was saved while it was
#     live);
#   • a vault entry is never overwritten in place — the previous copy rotates
#     into accounts/.history/<email>.<ts>.json first, so even a save that
#     lands under the wrong identity (a concurrent session can rewrite
#     .claude.json from memory) is recoverable rather than fatal;
#   • `use` saves the outgoing login before installing the incoming one.
acctdir="$SESSION_ACCOUNTS_DIR"
acct_cfg() { printf '%s' "$SESSION_CFG"; }
# The login NAME (lib: session_login_read) — the email, plus +<org slug> for a
# Team or Enterprise seat — is what the vault is keyed by. Read fresh, not
# memoised: `use` compares it before and after the swap in one process.
acct_live_login() { session_login_read; }
acct_name_of() { jq -r '.login // .email // empty' "$1"; }   # a vault entry's name
# Whether a credentials file or a vault entry carries a token that could
# authenticate. Absent, null and empty all answer no: `use` installs
# `.claudeAiOauth` wholesale, so an object with no token in it is dead weight
# whatever else it holds.
acct_token_ok() { jq -e '(.claudeAiOauth.accessToken // "") != ""' "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1; }

# Copy the live login into the vault. Idempotent: an unchanged entry is only
# touched (the mtime is the autosave's "already captured" marker).
acct_save() {
  local cfg login vf now
  cfg=$(acct_cfg); login=$(acct_live_login)
  [ "$login" != unknown ] && [ -s "$cfg/.credentials.json" ] || {
    echo "session account: nothing to save (no live login found)" >&2; return 1; }
  # A blank credential is not a credential. Five times in two months the live
  # file has held a structurally perfect claudeAiOauth object — every key
  # present, every type right — whose accessToken was the empty string, and once
  # a null claudeAiOauth landed in the vault a second after a healthy save.
  # Vaulting either overwrites the last working copy of the login, which is the
  # copy the recovery needs, so the save is refused instead.
  acct_token_ok "$cfg/.credentials.json" || {
    echo "session account: not saving $login — its live accessToken is empty, and vaulting that would destroy the last working copy of this login" >&2
    return 1; }
  mkdir -p "$acctdir" && chmod 700 "$acctdir"
  vf="$acctdir/$login.json"; now=$(date +%s)
  if [ -s "$vf" ]; then
    if jq -e --slurpfile v "$vf" '.claudeAiOauth == $v[0].claudeAiOauth' "$cfg/.credentials.json" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      touch "$vf"; return 0
    fi
    mkdir -p "$acctdir/.history" && chmod 700 "$acctdir/.history"
    cp -p "$vf" "$acctdir/.history/$login.$now.json"
  fi
  # `email` stays the raw address; `login` is the name the entry is filed under.
  jq -n --slurpfile c "$cfg/.credentials.json" --slurpfile j "$cfg/.claude.json" \
        --arg l "$login" --argjson t "$now" \
    '{email: ($j[0].oauthAccount.emailAddress // $l), login: $l,
      oauthAccount: $j[0].oauthAccount, claudeAiOauth: $c[0].claudeAiOauth, saved_at: $t}' \
    > "$acctdir/.save.$$" 2>/dev/null \
    && chmod 600 "$acctdir/.save.$$" && mv -f "$acctdir/.save.$$" "$vf"
}

# Vault entries in name order (C collation), which is what the list numbers:
# a stable order, so the number beside a login means the same login next time.
# Sorted on the name, not the filename: with the extension on, `+` (the
# organisation separator) sorts before `.json`, putting a seat above the plan
# it belongs to. `use` takes that row number, the whole name (which wins
# outright — the bare email is a prefix of its own Team seat's name, so no
# substring picks the plan when both are vaulted), or a unique case-insensitive
# substring.
acct_paths() {
  ls -1 "$acctdir"/*.json 2>/dev/null | sed 's/\.json$//' | LC_ALL=C sort | sed 's/$/.json/' || true
}
acct_resolve() {  # $1=row number | name | substring -> vault path on stdout
  local m="${1:-}" hits n p b lm
  case "$m" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) ;;
    *) n=$(acct_paths | grep -c . || true)
       p=$(acct_paths | sed -n "${m}p")
       [ "$m" != 0 ] && [ -n "$p" ] || {
         echo "session account: no login numbered $m (the list has $n)" >&2; return 1; }
       printf '%s\n' "$p"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  lm=$(printf '%s' "$m" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')
  hits=$(acct_paths | while IFS= read -r p; do
           b=${p##*/}; b=${b%.json}
           [ "$(printf '%s' "$b" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" = "$lm" ] && printf '%s\n' "$p"
         done)
  if [ -n "$m" ] && [ "$(printf '%s' "$hits" | grep -c . || true)" = 1 ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$hits"; return 0
  fi
  hits=$(acct_paths | while IFS= read -r p; do
           b=${p##*/}; b=${b%.json}
           printf '%s\n' "$b" | grep -qiF -- "$m" && printf '%s\n' "$p"
         done)
  n=$(printf '%s' "$hits" | grep -c . || true)
  if [ "$n" = 0 ]; then
    echo "session account: no saved login matches '$m'" >&2
    echo "  saved: $(acct_paths | sed 's|.*/||; s|\.json$||' | tr '\n' ' ')" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$n" -gt 1 ]; then
    echo "session account: '$m' matches $n logins:" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$hits" | sed 's|.*/||; s|\.json$||; s|^|    |' >&2
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$hits"
}

# Per-account limits, read from that login's statusline cache — the whole point
# of switching is "which login still has headroom", so `list` answers it, with
# each window's reset countdown in parentheses. A non-live login's cache stops
# refreshing the moment you switch away, so here a stale resets_at is the NORMAL
# case, and the two windows age differently (same asymmetry as the overview):
# the weekly runs on a fixed 7-day wall-clock cadence, so a past boundary steps
# forward (marked ~); the 5h is usage-anchored — it opens on that account's
# first request after an idle gap — so a past boundary says nothing about the
# next one and prints "?". Either way a passed boundary means the used% is not
# merely doubtful, it is KNOWN: usage dropped to 0 at the reset, so the % is
# projected to ~0 rather than showing the dead window's value (a floor — usage
# since the reset is invisible until that account's next render, hence the ~).
# A window the cache does not carry — the payload omits one now and then, and
# the statusline skips that segment — lists as n/a, the overview's word for it,
# not as the -1 sentinel with a % sign on it.
#
# Fable's weekly cap gets a column of its own, from a file of its own: the
# statusline payload carries only the 5h and all-models weekly windows, so it
# never reaches the last-status cache. It has no countdown column because it
# resets with the weekly (observed on every login, 2026-09-16), though a passed
# Fable reset still zeroes it the way a passed weekly one does. AGE is the
# row's OLDEST figure: the two files are refreshed on different schedules, and
# a row is only as current as its stalest number. A dash means no data at all.
acct_pct() { if [ "$1" = -1 ]; then printf 'n/a'; else printf '%s%%' "$1"; fi; }
acct_age() {  # SECONDS (-1 = no figure) -> - | now | Nm | Nh | Nd
  if   [ "$1" -lt 0 ];     then printf -- '-'
  elif [ "$1" -lt 120 ];   then printf 'now'
  elif [ "$1" -lt 3600 ];  then printf '%sm' $(( $1/60 ))
  elif [ "$1" -lt 86400 ]; then printf '%sh' $(( $1/3600 ))
  else                          printf '%sd' $(( $1/86400 )); fi
}
acct_week() {  # PCT RESETS_AT NOW -> "N%<TAB>left" for a fixed-cadence 7-day window
  local w="$1" rw="$2" now="$3" lw fw
  if (( rw > now )); then
    lw=$(( rw - now )); (( lw > WEEK_WINDOW )) && lw=$WEEK_WINDOW
    fw=$(dh "$lw")
  elif (( rw > 0 )); then
    lw=$(( rw + WEEK_WINDOW * ( (now - rw + WEEK_WINDOW - 1) / WEEK_WINDOW ) - now ))
    fw="~$(dh "$lw")"; w="~0"
  else
    fw="?"
  fi
  printf '%s\t%s' "$(acct_pct "$w")" "$fw"
}
# One login's figures as six tab-separated fields, none ever empty (IFS
# whitespace would collapse an empty one): 5h% 5h-left week% week-left fable% age.
acct_row() {
  local c fc f w rf rw fp fr now l5 f5 tab=$'\t' five week fable=n/a oldest=-1 a
  now=$(date +%s)
  five="-${tab}-"; week="-${tab}-"
  c=$(session_cache_path "$1")
  if [ -s "$c" ]; then
    IFS=$'\t' read -r f w rf rw < <(jq -r '[(.rate_limits.five_hour.used_percentage//-1),
                                            (.rate_limits.seven_day.used_percentage//-1),
                                            (.rate_limits.five_hour.resets_at//0),
                                            (.rate_limits.seven_day.resets_at//0)]|@tsv' "$c")
    f="${f%%.*}"; w="${w%%.*}"
    if (( rf > now )); then
      l5=$(( rf - now )); (( l5 > FIVE_WINDOW )) && l5=$FIVE_WINDOW
      f5=$(hm "$l5")
    else
      f5="?"; (( rf > 0 )) && f="~0"        # rf==0: no reset data at all, but the % is current
    fi
    five="$(acct_pct "$f")${tab}$f5"
    week=$(acct_week "$w" "$rw" "$now")
    oldest=$(( now - $(mtime_of "$c") ))
  fi
  fc=$(session_cache_path "$1" fable); fp=""
  [ -s "$fc" ] && IFS=$'\t' read -r fp fr < <(jq -r '[(.fable.used_percentage // -1), (.fable.resets_at // 0)] | @tsv' "$fc")
  if [ -n "$fp" ] && [ "$fp" != -1 ]; then
    fable=$(acct_week "${fp%%.*}" "${fr%%.*}" "$now"); fable=${fable%%"$tab"*}
    a=$(( now - $(mtime_of "$fc") )); (( a > oldest )) && oldest=$a
  fi
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$five" "$week" "$fable" "$(acct_age "$oldest")"
}
# The table's one format, header and rows alike, with the live marker first so
# it is where the eye starts. Every cell is ASCII: printf pads by bytes, so a
# multibyte glyph in a padded field would push the rest of its row out of line.
acct_line() {  # MARKER # LOGIN 5H RESET WEEK RESET FABLE AGE
  printf '  %-4s %3s  %-40s %-5s %-7s %-5s %-8s %-6s %s\n' "$@"
}
acct_header() { acct_line '' '#' LOGIN 5H RESET WEEK RESET FABLE AGE; }
acct_print_row() {  # MARKER NUMBER LOGIN
  local p5 r5 pw rw pf age
  IFS=$'\t' read -r p5 r5 pw rw pf age < <(acct_row "$3")
  acct_line "$1" "$2" "$3" "$p5" "$r5" "$pw" "$rw" "$pf" "$age"
}

# Refresh fable.<login>.json for every vaulted login whose access token is still
# good. The source is the usage endpoint behind /usage, whose limits[] carries
# Fable's cap as a `weekly_scoped` row scoped to the model (observed 2026-09-16:
# display_name "Fable", resets_at an ISO string with microseconds and +00:00).
#
# A lapsed token is skipped, never refreshed: a refresh rotates credentials that
# the live login's sessions may be holding, and nothing needs it — a login's
# Fable figure moves only while that login is used, so its last fetch stays
# right, and a passed reset is projected like the weekly's. The token reaches
# curl on stdin (-K -), never in argv, where any local user's ps can read it.
# One fetch per login, in parallel, 5 s each. Anything short of a Fable row
# with a numeric percent keeps the previous file: a failed request, a 200 with
# an error body, and a well-formed answer with no usable Fable row alike, since
# the endpoint is undocumented and absence there proves nothing. A host without
# curl lists every login's last figure.
ACCT_USAGE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage
ACCT_FABLE_JQ='
  if (.limits | type) != "array" then empty else
    first(.limits[]
          | select(.kind == "weekly_scoped"
                   and ((.scope.model.display_name // "") | ascii_downcase | startswith("fable"))
                   and (.percent | type) == "number"))
    | { fable: { used_percentage: .percent,
                 resets_at: ((.resets_at // "") | sub("\\.[0-9]+"; "") | sub("\\+00:00$"; "Z")
                             | try fromdateiso8601 catch 0) } }
  end'
acct_fetch_fable() {
  command -v curl >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  local now vf login tok c
  now=$(date +%s)
  while IFS= read -r vf; do
    IFS=$'\t' read -r login tok < <(jq -r --argjson now "$now" '
        select((.claudeAiOauth.expiresAt // 0) / 1000 > $now + 60)
        | [(.login // .email // ""), (.claudeAiOauth.accessToken // "")] | @tsv' "$vf") || continue
    [ -n "$login" ] && [ -n "$tok" ] || continue
    c=$(session_cache_path "$login" fable)
    # -e: a body without limits[] yields no output and exit 4, so it never
    # replaces the file; pipefail carries a curl failure through the same way.
    (
      printf 'header = "Authorization: Bearer %s"\n' "$tok" \
        | curl -sf -m 5 -K - -H 'anthropic-beta: oauth-2025-04-20' "$ACCT_USAGE_URL" \
        | jq -ce "$ACCT_FABLE_JQ" > "$c.tmp.$$" \
        && mv -f "$c.tmp.$$" "$c" || rm -f "$c.tmp.$$"
    ) &
  done < <(acct_paths)
  wait
}

# Install a vaulted login: the whole write sequence and nothing else. It prints
# nothing on any outcome — the status is the whole answer, so a caller with no
# human in front of it can use it, and one with a human keeps its own wording.
#   0  swapped
#   1  the credentials write failed; nothing was installed
#   2  the write reported success but the installed credential is not the
#      vault entry's, so the caller must not treat the login as switched.
#      The identity file is already written at that point, so it names the
#      login whose credential did not land: a caller that reports the failure
#      must not read the live login name back as the truth of what happened.
#   3  the entry cannot authenticate; nothing was read, written or vaulted
acct_swap() {  # VAULTFILE
  local vf="$1" cfg tmp
  cfg=$(acct_cfg)
  # Refused before the outgoing login is vaulted and before any write. An entry
  # with no usable token is the 2026-09-15 shape, and this is the path a human
  # takes while that outage is happening — reaching for another login and
  # installing a second dead credential over the one thing still working.
  acct_token_ok "$vf" || return 3
  acct_save 2>/dev/null || true   # never lose the login being replaced
  # Swap ONLY claudeAiOauth: .credentials.json also holds mcpOAuth (Granola,
  # DoneThat, …), which is not account-scoped and must survive the switch.
  tmp="$cfg/.credentials.swap.$$"
  jq --slurpfile v "$vf" '.claudeAiOauth = $v[0].claudeAiOauth' "$cfg/.credentials.json" > "$tmp" \
    && chmod 600 "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$cfg/.credentials.json" \
    || { rm -f "$tmp"; return 1; }
  # Identity too, so usage tracking labels the very next statusline render
  # correctly. The app re-fetches the profile on its own schedule, so this
  # is a head start, not the source of truth — and a concurrent session can
  # briefly clobber it back from memory.
  tmp="$cfg/.claude.swap.$$"
  jq --slurpfile v "$vf" '.oauthAccount = $v[0].oauthAccount' "$cfg/.claude.json" > "$tmp" \
    && mv -f "$tmp" "$cfg/.claude.json" || rm -f "$tmp"
  # Read the credential back out of the file the swap wrote. Not .claude.json
  # and not the login name it carries: that file is the one a concurrent session
  # rewrites from memory, so a name read from it can say "switched" over
  # credentials that never moved. A boolean comparison, so no token is rendered.
  jq -e --slurpfile v "$vf" '.claudeAiOauth == $v[0].claudeAiOauth' \
     "$cfg/.credentials.json" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
}

acct_main() {
  local sub="${1:-list}"; shift 2>/dev/null || true
  case "$sub" in
    list|"")
      acct_save 2>/dev/null || true
      local live vf name
      live=$(acct_live_login)
      [ -n "$(acct_paths)" ] || { echo "session account: no saved logins yet at $acctdir" >&2; return 1; }
      acct_fetch_fable
      local i=0
      acct_header
      while IFS= read -r vf; do
        [ -n "$vf" ] || continue
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        name=$(acct_name_of "$vf")
        acct_print_row "$([ "$name" = "$live" ] && printf live || true)" "$i" "$name"
      done < <(acct_paths)
      printf '\n  switch: session account use <#|name>   (next request, running sessions included)\n'
      ;;
    use)
      local m="${1:?usage: session account use <#|name>}" vf email cfg
      cfg=$(acct_cfg)
      # Where Claude Code keeps credentials in the macOS Keychain there is no
      # .credentials.json to swap. Refused first, before resolving or saving
      # anything, so the reason is the only thing that happens.
      [ -s "$cfg/.credentials.json" ] || {
        echo "session account use: this Claude Code stores credentials in the macOS Keychain, which this build cannot swap; see README.md" >&2
        return 1; }
      vf=$(acct_resolve "$m") || return 1
      email=$(acct_name_of "$vf")
      if [ "$email" = "$(acct_live_login)" ]; then
        echo "session account: already on $email"; return 0
      fi
      acct_swap "$vf"
      case $? in
        1) echo "session account: failed to write credentials" >&2; return 1 ;;
        2) echo "session account: the credential swap did not land — the live credential is not $email's; check $cfg/.credentials.json" >&2; return 1 ;;
        3) echo "session account use: $email's vaulted accessToken is empty — installing it would leave nothing on this machine able to authenticate; run /login instead" >&2; return 1 ;;
      esac
      # After the swap, never before: a slow endpoint must not stand between a
      # capped session and the login that frees it. The outgoing login's Fable
      # figure stops moving now, so this is the fetch that keeps it right.
      acct_fetch_fable
      printf 'Switched to %s\n' "$email"
      acct_header
      acct_print_row live "$(acct_paths | grep -nxF -- "$vf" | cut -d: -f1)" "$email"
      local n; n=$(pgrep -x -u "$(id -u)" claude 2>/dev/null | grep -c . || true)
      if [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
        printf 'Takes effect on the next request — including the %s session(s) already running,\n' "$n"
        printf 'which share this config dir. Their displayed %%s catch up one turn later.\n'
      else
        printf 'Takes effect on the next request.\n'
      fi
      ;;
    save)
      # acct_save says on stderr why it refused; a second, guessed reason here
      # would contradict it.
      acct_save && printf 'Saved %s to %s\n' "$(acct_live_login)" "$acctdir" || return 1
      ;;
    rm)
      local vf; vf=$(acct_resolve "${1:?usage: session account rm <name>}") || return 1
      [ "$(acct_name_of "$vf")" = "$(acct_live_login)" ] && {
        echo "session account: refusing to remove the live login" >&2; return 1; }
      mkdir -p "$acctdir/.history" && mv -f "$vf" "$acctdir/.history/$(basename "$vf" .json).$(date +%s).json"
      echo "Removed (kept a copy under $acctdir/.history/)"
      ;;
    -h|--help)
      cat <<'EOF'
Usage: session account [list | use <#|name> | save | rm <name>]
  list           saved logins, numbered in name order, each with its rate-limit
                 headroom and reset countdowns (default). A non-live login's snapshot ages from
                 the moment you switch away; once a window's reset passes, its
                 used% shows ~0 — usage zeroes at the boundary, and the real
                 value returns on that account's next render. The weekly's
                 next reset is projected forward (~); the 5h is usage-anchored
                 and prints "?" — it re-anchors on the account's next request.
                 FABLE is Fable's own weekly cap, which resets with WEEK. It is
                 fetched from the usage endpoint with each login's token while
                 that token is unexpired (never refreshed); a login whose token
                 lapsed shows its last fetch. Needs curl; n/a when never fetched.
                 AGE is the age of the row's oldest figure; - means no data
  use <#|name>   switch the live login in place. Takes a row number from the
                 list, the whole login name (always wins), or a unique
                 case-insensitive substring of it. A login is named by its
                 email; a Team or Enterprise seat on the same email is
                 <email>+<org slug>, a separate login with its own credentials
                 and limits. Takes effect on the next request,
                 for running sessions too — no restart, no re-auth. The
                 switch is box-wide: every claude here shares the config dir
  save           snapshot the live login into the vault (the statusline
                 does this automatically every time credentials change)
  rm <name>      drop a saved login (a copy stays in accounts/.history/)

To ADD a login, run `/login` in a session — there is no `add` subcommand and
none is needed: the statusline vaults whatever you log into, within a render.
`/login` is also non-destructive now, because the login it replaces was itself
vaulted while live, so `session account use <old>` brings it back with no
re-authentication. Note both `/login` and `use` are box-wide: every claude here
shares this config dir, so all running sessions move to the new login too.
EOF
      ;;
    *) echo "session account: unknown subcommand '$sub' (list|use|save|rm)" >&2; return 2 ;;
  esac
}
[ "${1:-}" = account ] && { shift; acct_main "$@"; exit $?; }

# -- Wiring check (`session doctor`) ------------------------------------------
# Effect-based: every check asks whether the thing works HERE, on this machine,
# and prints the fact it decided on, so a failure names what to change rather
# than that something is wrong. Three states, because "not yet" and "wired
# wrong" are different problems with different remedies:
#   ok       it works, with the evidence beside it
#   pending  nothing has produced it yet — usually "interact once", not a bug
#   FAIL     wired wrong, and this says what is wrong
# Exit 1 if anything FAILed; pending never fails the run.
DOC_FAIL=0
doc() { case "$1" in FAIL) DOC_FAIL=1 ;; esac; printf '  %-7s  %-11s %s\n' "$1" "$2" "$3"; }

doctor_main() {
  case "${1:-}" in
    ""|-h|--help)
      [ -n "${1:-}" ] && { echo "Usage: session doctor   (no arguments)"; exit 0; } ;;
    *) echo "session doctor: unknown arg '$1' (it takes none)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  local src m cache_f j jrc f v sl slsrc sl_ours cfg_bad last lr sid t r tot armed \
        newest_s have_e plat miss age crdl

  echo "-- session doctor -------------------------------------------------"
  echo "  code     $SESSION_HOME"

  # 1. The data root, and where the path came from. A wrong root is the failure
  #    this whole check exists for: producer and consumer agreeing on an empty
  #    directory looks exactly like a quiet machine.
  # Which of the two set it cannot be known here: sourcing session.conf sets
  # SESSION_DATA_DIR, so by the time anything can look, a conf value and an
  # environment value are the same variable. The old wording picked one and was
  # wrong in exactly the case worth running the doctor for — a conf naming one
  # root while the environment names another. Name the candidates instead; the
  # resolved root is printed beside them, which is what the check is for.
  src="the default under the config dir"
  if [ "$SESSION_DATA" != "$SESSION_CFG/session-usage" ]; then
    src="from SESSION_DATA_DIR"
    [ -r "$SESSION_CFG/session.conf" ] && grep -q 'SESSION_DATA_DIR' "$SESSION_CFG/session.conf" \
      && src="$src, which $SESSION_CFG/session.conf also sets"
  fi
  if [ ! -d "$SESSION_DATA" ]; then
    doc pending "data root" "$SESSION_DATA does not exist yet — $src; install.sh creates it"
  elif [ ! -w "$SESSION_DATA" ]; then
    doc FAIL "data root" "$SESSION_DATA is not writable by this user — $src"
  else
    m=$(stat -c %a "$SESSION_DATA" 2>/dev/null || stat -f %Lp "$SESSION_DATA" 2>/dev/null)
    if [ "$m" = 700 ]; then
      doc ok "data root" "$SESSION_DATA (mode 700, $src)"
    else
      doc FAIL "data root" "$SESSION_DATA is mode ${m:-?}, not 700 — it holds login-keyed data; chmod 700 it"
    fi
  fi

  # 2. What `session` on PATH actually runs. Two copies of this file on one
  #    machine is the other way producer and consumer part company.
  t=$(command -v session 2>/dev/null || true)
  if [ -z "$t" ]; then
    doc pending "on PATH" "no \`session\` on PATH — install.sh links one into your bindir"
  else
    r=$(realpath_of "$t")
    if [ "$r" = "$SESSION_HOME/session" ]; then
      doc ok "on PATH" "$t"
    else
      doc FAIL "on PATH" "$t runs $r, not $SESSION_HOME/session — two copies would disagree about everything"
    fi
  fi

  # 3. The two files this install is configured by. Both are read by something
  #    that swallows errors — jq under 2>/dev/null below, and `.` from the lib at
  #    source time — so a broken one shows up everywhere as things being
  #    mysteriously absent rather than as a parse error anyone sees.
  cfg_bad=0
  f="$SESSION_CFG/settings.json"
  if [ ! -e "$f" ]; then
    doc pending "settings" "no $f yet — install.sh creates it"
  elif [ ! -r "$f" ]; then
    # jq cannot tell unreadable from unparseable, and "not valid JSON" sends
    # the reader to edit a file whose contents are fine.
    cfg_bad=1
    doc FAIL "settings" "$f exists but cannot be read — fix its permissions"
  elif jq -e . "$f" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    doc ok "settings" "$f parses"
  else
    cfg_bad=1
    doc FAIL "settings" "$f is not valid JSON — Claude Code and every check below read it as empty"
  fi
  cf="$SESSION_CFG/session.conf"
  if [ -e "$cf" ]; then
    if [ ! -r "$cf" ]; then
      doc FAIL "session.conf" "$cf exists but cannot be read — fix its permissions"
    elif ! _session_conf_trusted "$cf" 2>/dev/null; then
      # The lib skips it and says so only on a stderr the statusline discards;
      # this line is the one place the skip is named where someone looks.
      cmode=$(stat -c %a "$cf" 2>/dev/null || stat -f %Lp "$cf" 2>/dev/null)
      doc FAIL "session.conf" "$cf is skipped by the lib (mode ${cmode:-unknown}; it must be owned by you and neither group- nor world-writable) — every path silently falls back to its default; chmod 600 it"
    elif ! bash -n "$cf" 2>/dev/null; then
      doc FAIL "session.conf" "$cf is not valid shell — the lib sources it, so every path silently falls back to its default"
    fi
  fi

  # The EFFECTIVE statusLine, not the one in the user settings: a project
  # settings file in the directory a session runs from silently wins, and a
  # session started there would render someone else's statusline (and write no
  # cache here). Resolved now because the cache check below needs to know
  # whether a statusline of ours is even supposed to be running.
  sl=""; slsrc=""; sl_ours=0
  for f in "$PWD/.claude/settings.local.json" "$PWD/.claude/settings.json" "$SESSION_CFG/settings.json"; do
    [ -r "$f" ] || continue
    [ "$f" = "$SESSION_CFG/settings.json" ] && [ "$cfg_bad" = 1 ] && continue
    v=$(jq -r '.statusLine.command // empty' "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)
    [ -n "$v" ] && { sl="$v"; slsrc="$f"; break; }
  done
  if [ -n "$sl" ]; then
    last=${sl##* }
    lr=$(realpath_of "$last")
    [ "$lr" = "$SESSION_HOME/statusline.sh" ] && sl_ours=1
  fi

  # 4. The statusline's cache: the only thing the statusline produces that the
  #    CLI reads, so its absence and its age are both diagnostic.
  cache_f=$(session_cache_path)
  if [ -s "$cache_f" ]; then
    if ! jq -e '.rate_limits' "$cache_f" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      doc FAIL "cache" "$cache_f does not parse, or carries no rate_limits"
    else
      age=$(( $(now_epoch) - $(mtime_of "$cache_f") ))
      v=$(jq -r '.version // "unknown"' "$cache_f" 2>/dev/null)
      if [ -n "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" ] && [ "$age" -gt 120 ]; then
        doc FAIL "cache" "${age}s old inside a live session — the statusline is not rendering (Claude Code $v)"
      else
        doc ok "cache" "${age}s old, Claude Code $v (${cache_f##*/})"
      fi
    fi
  elif [ -s "$SESSION_TLOG" ] || [ -s "$SESSION_SLOG" ]; then
    # Rows arriving with no cache means one of two things, and only one of them
    # is broken: a statusline of ours is configured and is not rendering (FAIL),
    # or this install deliberately has the hooks and no statusline — a state the
    # README describes and supports, where the limit tiers simply stay
    # unavailable. A permanent FAIL on the second would train the reader to
    # ignore the check.
    if [ "$sl_ours" = 1 ]; then
      doc FAIL "cache" "no $cache_f, and $slsrc runs our statusline over a root that is taking rows — it is wired and not rendering"
    else
      doc pending "cache" "no $cache_f — the hooks write here and no statusline of ours is configured; the limit tiers stay unavailable"
    fi
  else
    doc pending "cache" "no $cache_f yet — nothing has run against this root; interact once"
  fi

  # 5. The turn log. A log with ends and no starts is the exact shape of a
  #    UserPromptSubmit hook that is not firing, and it reads downstream as a
  #    worked day with no work in it.
  if [ ! -s "$SESSION_TLOG" ]; then
    doc pending "turn log" "no rows at $SESSION_TLOG yet — the lifecycle hooks append it"
  else
    newest_s=$(awk -F'\t' '$3=="s"{t=$1+0} END{printf "%d\n", t+0}' "$SESSION_TLOG")
    have_e=$(awk -F'\t' '$3=="e"{n++} END{printf "%d\n", n+0}' "$SESSION_TLOG")
    if [ "$newest_s" = 0 ] && [ "$have_e" != 0 ]; then
      doc FAIL "turn log" "$have_e turn ends and no starts — the UserPromptSubmit --hook entry is missing"
    elif [ "$newest_s" = 0 ]; then
      doc pending "turn log" "no turn starts logged yet — submit a prompt"
    else
      age=$(( $(now_epoch) - newest_s ))
      if [ "$age" -le 86400 ]; then
        doc ok "turn log" "newest turn start $(( age / 60 ))m ago"
      else
        doc ok "turn log" "newest turn start $(( age / 86400 ))d ago — nothing has run here since"
      fi
    fi
  fi

  # 6. The contract a task logger reads. Checked by running it, not by reading
  #    the code that writes it.
  j=$(bash "$SESSION_HOME/session" time --json 2>/dev/null); jrc=$?
  if [ -z "$j" ]; then
    # Empty output has several causes and this must not assert the wrong one:
    # `time --json` also exits 1 when it cannot identify a session, which has
    # nothing to do with the turn log — and is the normal case in exactly the
    # non-interactive runs the doctor is most likely to see (the installer's
    # last step, cron, CI).
    if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      doc FAIL "time --json" "jq is not on PATH, and every JSON path here needs it"
    elif [ ! -s "$SESSION_TLOG" ] && [ ! -s "$SESSION_ARCHIVE/${SESSION_TLOG##*/}" ]; then
      doc pending "time --json" "no figures yet — nothing has written a turn log to report on"
    elif ! resolve_sid >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      doc pending "time --json" "could not identify a session to report on — run this from inside one"
    else
      doc FAIL "time --json" "produced nothing (exit $jrc) over a turn log that has rows — run \`session time --json\` to see why"
    fi
  elif printf '%s' "$j" | jq -e '(.date|type=="string") and (.attended_s|type=="number")
        and ((.attended_s|floor) == .attended_s) and (.active_s|type=="number")
        and ((.active_s|floor) == .active_s)
        and (.attended_basis == "focus" or .attended_basis == "active")' >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    doc ok "time --json" "$(printf '%s' "$j" | jq -r '"date \(.date) · active \(.active_s)s · attended \(.attended_s)s (\(.attended_basis))"')"
  else
    doc FAIL "time --json" "does not satisfy the contract a task logger reads: $j"
  fi

  # 7. What the statusLine resolved to above.
  if [ -z "$sl" ]; then
    if [ "$cfg_bad" = 1 ]; then
      doc FAIL "statusLine" "cannot be read — $SESSION_CFG/settings.json does not parse"
    else
      doc pending "statusLine" "none configured for this directory — install.sh writes one"
    fi
  elif [ ! -e "${sl##* }" ]; then
    doc FAIL "statusLine" "$slsrc runs \`$sl\`, and ${sl##* } does not exist"
  elif [ "$sl_ours" = 1 ]; then
    doc ok "statusLine" "$slsrc"
  else
    doc FAIL "statusLine" "$slsrc runs \`$sl\` ($(realpath_of "${sl##* }")), not $SESSION_HOME/statusline.sh"
  fi

  # 8. Identity. Both halves matter: the env var is what every consumer keys on,
  #    and resolve_sid is what this CLI answers with.
  if [ -z "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" ]; then
    doc pending "session id" "not inside a Claude Code session — run this from one to check identity"
  else
    sid=$(resolve_sid || true)
    if [ "$sid" != "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" ]; then
      doc FAIL "session id" "whoami answers ${sid:-<nothing>}, the environment says $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID"
    elif printf '%s' "$sid" | grep -qE '^[0-9a-f]{8}(-[0-9a-f]{4}){3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$'; then
      doc ok "session id" "$sid"
    else
      doc FAIL "session id" "'$sid' is not a session UUID"
    fi
  fi

  # 9. The auto-resume waiter. An entry without asyncRewake is worse than none:
  #    the harness runs it synchronously, so a 700s hook blocks every prompt.
  f="$SESSION_CFG/settings.json"
  if [ ! -e "$f" ]; then
    doc pending "auto-resume" "no $f yet"
  elif [ ! -r "$f" ]; then
    doc FAIL "auto-resume" "$f exists but cannot be read — fix its permissions"
  elif [ "$cfg_bad" = 1 ]; then
    doc FAIL "auto-resume" "cannot be read — $f does not parse"
  else
    tot=$(jq '[.hooks[]?[]?.hooks[]? | select(.command? // "" | test("rewake-waiter"))] | length' "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    armed=$(jq '[.hooks[]?[]?.hooks[]? | select((.command? // "" | test("rewake-waiter")) and .asyncRewake == true)] | length' "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    if [ "${tot:-0}" = 0 ]; then
      doc pending "auto-resume" "off — no --rewake-waiter entry (install.sh --no-rewake leaves it that way)"
    elif [ "${armed:-0}" = "${tot:-0}" ]; then
      doc ok "auto-resume" "$armed entr(y/ies), asyncRewake armed"
    else
      doc FAIL "auto-resume" "$tot rewake entr(y/ies), only $armed with asyncRewake — the rest run synchronously and block the prompt"
    fi
  fi

  # 10. What this host can and cannot do. perl and jq are load-bearing; the rest
  #    have fallbacks, and naming them is how a missing feature stops looking
  #    like a bug.
  plat=""; miss=""
  for t in perl jq; do
    if command -v "$t" >/dev/null 2>&1; then plat="$plat$t present · "; else plat="$plat$t MISSING · "; miss="$miss $t"; fi
  done
  command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    && plat="${plat}tmux present · " || plat="${plat}tmux absent (no focus tracking, no reboot/resume) · "
  command -v inotifywait >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    && plat="${plat}inotifywait present · " || plat="${plat}inotifywait absent (waits poll at 15s) · "
  command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    && plat="${plat}flock(1) · " || plat="${plat}no flock(1) (perl flock) · "
  [ -d /proc/self ] && plat="$plat/proc" || plat="${plat}no /proc (ps)"
  if [ -n "$miss" ]; then doc FAIL platform "$plat"; else doc ok platform "$plat"; fi
  if [ "$(uname -s 2>/dev/null)" = Darwin ]; then
    doc pending platform "macOS: \`session account\` cannot swap the login (credentials are in the Keychain)"
  fi

  # A blank live credential. Not one of the numbered checks: it reports a state
  # the machine is in rather than how it is wired, and says nothing when the
  # machine is not in it. The condition is "proven blank" rather than "not
  # proven good", because an unreadable or unparseable credentials file is a
  # different fault and this line would misname it.
  if [ -s "$SESSION_CFG/.credentials.json" ] \
     && jq -e '(.claudeAiOauth.accessToken // "") == ""' "$SESSION_CFG/.credentials.json" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    crdl=$(acct_live_login)
    # The entry has to carry a token of its own before it can be called the
    # copy to restore. Entries blanked before this refusal existed are still in
    # vaults, and `use` installs whatever the entry holds.
    if acct_token_ok "$acctdir/$crdl.json"; then
      doc FAIL credentials "the live accessToken is empty — nothing can authenticate; $acctdir/$crdl.json is kept as the last working copy, restore it with \`session account use $crdl\`"
    else
      doc FAIL credentials "the live accessToken is empty — nothing can authenticate, and no vaulted copy of $crdl carries a usable token; re-run /login"
    fi
  fi

  echo "-------------------------------------------------------------------"
  if [ "$DOC_FAIL" = 0 ]; then
    echo "  nothing is wired wrong."
    exit 0
  fi
  echo "  see the FAIL lines above; README.md has the install steps."
  exit 1
}
[ "${1:-}" = doctor ] && { shift; doctor_main "$@"; }

# tmux client-focus plumbing ("--focus-mark in|out CLIENT" — tmux hook args, not
# stdin JSON): one self-contained row per focus flank, for `session time`
# attended-time. Row: ms-timestamp, in|out, client, tmux session (the stable
# vsc-<window> tab identity), pane, Claude session id. sid comes from the
# statusline's pane map at log time, so later pane id recycling can't rewrite
# history. ms precision keeps rapid switches ordered (the hooks run async via
# run-shell -b). Always exits 0.
#
# CLIENT must come from #{hook_client}, NOT #{client_name}: in a per-client hook
# #{client_name} resolves to the command queue's *current* client, which is some
# other arbitrary attached client (verified on tmux 3.4 — a focus-in on pts/8
# logged pts/10, a focus-in on pts/10 logged pts/8). That scrambled every flank:
# a client's "out" landed on a client that never opened a span while its own
# span stayed open to day end, so 19 clients ended 2026-07-27 with a dangling
# "in" worth 6.6-15.3 h each (246 h of raw spans in a 24 h day). #{hook_session}
# and #{hook_pane} are EMPTY for client hooks, so session and pane are resolved
# from the client below instead.
if [ "${1:-}" = --focus-mark ]; then
  # A host without tmux has no client focus to track. Exit 0 and say nothing:
  # this runs from a tmux hook and a cron line, neither of which has a reader.
  command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
  ev="${2:-}"; fcl="${3:--}"
  # $1=ev $2=client [$3=ts] [$4=session] [$5=pane] — session/pane are resolved
  # from the client when not supplied. list-clients -f expands the format once
  # per client, so what it reports is the session/pane THAT client is viewing;
  # display-message -c does NOT scope format expansion (verified: every -c
  # returns the globally-current client's pane), so it cannot be used here.
  flog_row() {
    local ev="$1" cl="${2:--}" ts="${3:-}" sess="${4:-}" pn="${5:-}" sid="-"
    [ -n "$ts" ] || ts=$(epoch_ms)
    # epoch_ms is the one perl call on this path, and a host with tmux and
    # without perl would otherwise write a row stamped with nothing. Every
    # reader filters on $1+0, which reads an empty field as epoch 0: the flank
    # is dropped from attended time, and at the head of the file it also
    # convinces log_srcs that the live log covers every request. No row is the
    # better answer, and this is a hook, so it stays silent either way.
    [ -n "$ts" ] || return 0
    if { [ -z "$sess" ] || [ -z "$pn" ]; } && [ "$cl" != "-" ]; then
      # quiet: the client is already gone on detach, and list-clients then
      # prints nothing (empty sess/pn below) rather than a row
      IFS=$'\t' read -r sess pn < <(tmux list-clients -f "#{==:#{client_name},$cl}" \
        -F "#{client_session}"$'\t'"#{pane_id}" 2>/dev/null) || true
    fi
    [ -n "$sess" ] || sess="-"
    [ -n "$pn" ] || pn="-"
    [ "$pn" != "-" ] && [ -s "$panedir/${pn#%}" ] && sid=$(cat "$panedir/${pn#%}")
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$ts" "$ev" "$cl" "$sess" "$pn" "${sid:--}" >> "$flog"
  }
  case "$ev" in
    in|out) flog_row "$ev" "$fcl" ;;
    # "switch SESSION": that session's current window changed (prefix-switch
    # inside an attach-style client — VS Code attach-main, termux/et). Only
    # meaningful when a FOCUSED client is viewing that session: log an "in",
    # which supersedes the client's previous span in the reader. Unfocused/
    # background switches log nothing.
    # NB the focused test is `#{m:*focused*,#{client_flags}}`. There is no
    # client_focused format in tmux 3.4 — it expands to empty, so the
    # `#{?client_focused,...}` this used to use was false for every client and
    # the whole handler was a silent no-op. `focused` appears in client_flags.
    switch)
      fts="${3:--}"
      fcl=$(tmux list-clients -t "$fts" -f '#{m:*focused*,#{client_flags}}' -F '#{client_name}' 2>/dev/null | awk 'NF {print; exit}')
      [ -n "$fcl" ] && flog_row in "$fcl" "" "$fts" ;;
    # "tick" (cron, 1/min): an "act" row for each client that had input within
    # the last ~90s — focused or not. Keystrokes update tmux's client_activity,
    # and so does scrolling (mouse mode makes wheel events input). Desktop
    # terminals can only receive input while focused, so this is equivalent to
    # focused-only there; Termux flaps 1004 focus around the soft keyboard and
    # keeps typing while "blurred" (verified: input 21s after a focus-out), so
    # activity is the truer attention signal on the phone. The reader caps
    # attended spans at last-activity + grace and turns standalone acts into
    # attended windows, so an abandoned focused tab still stops accruing.
    # Rows are stamped with client_activity — the ACTUAL input time — not tick
    # time: a tab you left <90s ago still passes the recency test, and a
    # tick-time stamp would land after its out flank, minting a phantom
    # standalone window for a tab you already left (observed: 194 such acts in
    # one day ≈ hours of phantom attended). With the true input time, stale
    # desktop acts fall inside their span (absorbed by clipping) while Termux
    # blur-typing still lands in the gap where it really happened.
    # The recency window is the caller's, because it belongs to the SAMPLING
    # RATE, not to this code: it has to be at least one tick plus slack, or
    # input between two ticks goes unseen. 90 fits the 1/min cron it was
    # written for; a 10s timer passes ~20 and stops re-recording the same
    # client_activity six times a minute.
    tick)
      nowi=$(date +%s); maxage="${3:-90}"
      fmt=$'#{client_name}\t#{client_session}\t#{pane_id}\t#{client_activity}'
      tmux list-clients -F "$fmt" 2>/dev/null | while IFS=$'\t' read -r c s p a; do
        [ -n "$a" ] && [ $(( nowi - a )) -lt "$maxage" ] && flog_row act "$c" "$a" "$s" "$p"
      done ;;
  esac
  exit 0
fi

# Hook plumbing: append one lifecycle event row to the turn log and exit 0
# unconditionally with no output — several of these events treat exit 2 as
# "block" (Stop: keep going; SubagentStop: don't stop; PostCompact is safe but
# uniformity is cheaper than remembering which is which).
# Row: ts  sid  ev  prompt_id  detail  agent_id   ("-" = not applicable):
#   s turn start (appended by --hook)      e turn end (Stop)
#   f turn failed (StopFailure; detail=error type, e.g. rate_limit)
#   x session end (SessionEnd; detail=reason)
#   a/z subagent start/stop (detail=agent type; agent_id pairs them)
#   c compaction (PostCompact; detail=manual|auto)
#   p permission prompt shown (Notification; a within-turn wait-on-human marker)
# The detail extractor is one //-chain across the per-event field names — for
# any given event all but its own field are absent.
case "${1:-}" in
  --turn-end|--turn-fail|--session-end|--subagent-start|--subagent-end|--compact-mark|--perm-mark)
    if [ ! -t 0 ]; then
      IFS=$'\t' read -r esid epid edet eaid < <(jq -r \
        '[(.session_id//"-"),(.prompt_id//"-"),
          (.error_type//.error//.reason//.compaction_reason//.agent_type//"-"),
          (.agent_id//"-")]|@tsv' 2>/dev/null || true)
      if [ -n "${esid:-}" ] && [ "$esid" != "-" ]; then
        case "$1" in
          --turn-end) ev=e ;;      --turn-fail) ev=f ;;     --session-end) ev=x ;;
          --subagent-start) ev=a ;; --subagent-end) ev=z ;; --compact-mark) ev=c ;;
          --perm-mark) ev=p ;;
        esac
        # The brace group carries the stderr redirect: a redirect that fails is
        # reported when it is set up, so a trailing 2>/dev/null on the same
        # command is too late (measured). A hook has no reader for that error,
        # and `session doctor` diagnoses the missing root properly.
        { printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' \
            "$(date +%s)" "$esid" "$ev" "${epid:--}" "${edet:--}" "${eaid:--}" >> "$tlog"
        } 2>/dev/null
      fi
    fi
    # The logs stay bounded whether or not the statusline is installed: this is
    # the second producer, behind the same once-a-day marker as the first.
    [ "$1" = --session-end ] && session_prune_daily
    exit 0 ;;
esac

mode=text; waitwin=five; submode=""; target_sid=""; all=0; yday=0; datearg=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --compact|--oneline) mode=compact ;;
    --hook)  mode=hook ;;
    --json)  mode=json ;;
    --guard) mode=guard ;;
    --wait)  mode='wait'
             case "${2:-}" in 5h|five) waitwin=five; shift ;; week|weekly|7d) waitwin=week; shift ;;
                              guard|pace) waitwin=guard; shift ;; esac ;;
    --rewake-waiter) mode=rewake ;;  # asyncRewake hook plumbing (see the rewake block)
    usage)   submode=usage
             if [ $# -gt 1 ] && [ "${2#-}" = "${2:-}" ]; then target_sid="$2"; shift; fi ;;
    time)    submode='time' ;;
    --all)   all=1 ;;
    --yesterday) yday=1 ;;
    --date)  shift; datearg="${1:?--date needs a date, e.g. 2026-08-28}" ;;
    --spans) spansout=1 ;;   # machine-readable attended spans, for external analysis
    --tail)   shift; ATTEND_TAIL="${1:?--tail needs seconds}"; tailset=1 ;;
    --bridge) shift; ATTEND_GRACE="${1:?--bridge needs seconds}"
              [ -n "${SESSION_ATTEND_TAIL:-}" ] || [ -n "${tailset:-}" ] || ATTEND_TAIL="$ATTEND_GRACE" ;;
    --from)  shift; rangefrom="${1:?--from needs a date}" ;;
    --to)    shift; rangeto="${1:?--to needs a date}" ;;
    --file)  shift; cache="${1:?--file needs a path}" ;;
    -h|--help)
      cat <<'HELP'
session - identity, plan-limit usage, and per-turn time for Claude Code sessions

Overview:
  session                          id · name, 5h/weekly limit bars + reset
                                   countdowns, context fill, this session's share
  session doctor                   check this install end to end: data root, the
                                   CLI on PATH, cache, turn log, the effective
                                   statusLine, hooks, platform. ok / pending /
                                   FAIL; only a FAIL exits 1

Identity (pane-safe: reports the session actually invoking it):
  session whoami                   two lines: id + auto-title
  session whoami --id              just the UUID
                                   (resume: agent <dir> -r "$(session whoami --id)")
  session whoami --name            just the auto-title
  session whoami --json            {"id": "...", "name": "..."}

Lookup (any session, both directions; 8-char table ids work as prefixes):
  session name <id|prefix>         that session's title
  session id <title-substring>     that session's id (case-insensitive; several
                                   matches print "id<TAB>name" lines instead)
  session peers [--json]           live sessions on this box: cross-session
                                   address (ListAgents name = SendMessage `to:`)
                                   ↔ id + title; REACH = visible to ListAgents.
                                   --json carries full session ids

Usage attribution (who is burning the shared 5h/weekly caps):
  session usage [ID]               one session's counted $ burn + estimated share
                                   of each window (default: the invoking session)
  session usage --all              every tracked session, sorted by 5h spend
  session usage [ID|--all] --json  raw fields

Time tracking (hook-fed turn boundaries; gaps between turns never count):
  session time                     today: closed turns, active time, watched +
                                   attended (focus), waits (this session)
  session time --all               the same, one row per session
  session time ... --yesterday     the same over yesterday's full day
  session time ... --date D        the same over any past day: YYYY-MM-DD or
                                   'yesterday'; free text ("3 days ago") needs
                                   GNU date and is refused without it
  session time ... --spans         the attended intervals themselves, as
                                   "sid<TAB>start<TAB>end" epoch seconds
  session time --from A --to B     attended time per day over a span of days,
                                   plus the total; one sort of the log for the
                                   whole range, and --spans works here too
  session time ... --tail S        seconds credited after the last interaction
                                   of a stretch (default: the bridge). A silence
                                   shorter than the bridge still joins two
                                   interactions either way.
  session time ... --bridge S      silence still counted as one stretch
                                   (default SESSION_ATTEND_GRACE); sets the tail
                                   with it unless --tail says otherwise
  session time --json              this session's figures as one JSON object
                                   (attended_s is what a task logger reads)

Accounts (several subscription logins, one config dir; `session account -h`):
  session account                  saved logins + each one's rate-limit headroom
